# P1 report — the work as I left it when the host killed the run

## 1. Was P1 finished? No — one proof leg is unresolved, and it may be red

The code and tests were in the state the orchestrator verified, and clj and cljs were green under my own runs. But **the cljd lane was not green at my last complete observation, and I was killed while identifying why.** Specifically, in sequence:

- First `bb test:cljd` run: exit code 1, output truncated by the tool before the summary.
- Second run (failure-filtered): ended `01:46 +1295 -6: Some tests failed.` — **six failures, names never obtained.** The `-6` counter was already present early in the visible stream, so the failures occurred before the namespaces shown in my tail; I have no information about which tests or namespaces they are in.
- Third run (grepping for the `[E]` failure lines): this is the run the host killed — exit 137, no output.

So the honest state of the proof: **clj 1430/165264 green, cljs 1349/34882 green with `dao.jing.file-test` confirmed, cljd = 6 unidentified failures, attribution unknown.** I cannot say whether those six are mine. Two things matter for whoever triages: (a) I never personally observed the cljd lane green on this revision *before* my change — the "1290 → 1299 all passed" baseline was reported to me in the P0 review, not run by me — so pre-existing or flaky-lane failures (e.g. cross-host peer tests) are not excluded; (b) if they are mine, suspect #1 is in my test helpers, not the backend (see §5d). When the lane frees, the attribution step is exactly what my third run was attempting: run the lane, grep `[E]`, check whether any failure names `dao.jing.file-test`.

Beyond that, the only unfinished item was the report you are now reading.

