Deliberately not re-tested here, with the reason: **D1** (plain-data handle, missing-fn throws) and **B1–B3** (`materialize!` derivation, verdict vocabulary, `:present` read-back) are the A–D core group's, pinned by the untouched `jing_test`, which ran green and unchanged — re-pinning them in `file_test` would test the core twice and the backend zero additional times. **D4** is dropped per the plan's own `[T✗]` — the atom shape is not pinned; `records` is the file backend's test view, exactly as D4 directs. **E-group** (pool drains, convergence) is P2's; the v1 `observer-convergence-test` was deliberately not carried into the new file.

## 4. What I could not do

- **(a) Complete the cljd proof.** Two hosts green, one unresolved with six observed failures (§1). Until those are attributed, P1's "all three hosts" requirement is not met — adopt the artifact as *code-complete, Dart-unverified*.
- **(b) Test F2's unequal-collision branch as written in the brief.** It is unreachable through valid frames: B6 forces a frame's address to hash its payload, so two *individually valid* frames at one address are necessarily equal. The brief's "collision on replay fails the open" is satisfied, but on F4's ground — the second frame's hash check fires before the accumulation's collision branch, which is retained as defensive depth and is untestable by construction. The test's docstring says this; flagging it here because it is the one letter-of-the-brief deviation.
- **(c) Observe F1's flush directly.** "Acknowledged only after the write is flushed" has no portable observable at the syscall level; test 5 is the consequence proxy (an acknowledged insert survives an immediate close) — the same evidence shape the v1 suite used.
- **(d) Contention off the JVM.** cljs/cljd are single-threaded; those arms assert-true with a message, as the v1 test did.

## 5. What a reviewer will not see in the diff

- **(a) A rejected framing alternative.** I considered and rejected a magic/version file header: the canonical-encoding migration (dao.jing.md's first open item) changes every minted address wholesale, so re-materialization is required regardless and frame versioning buys nothing. The chosen shape — 4-byte big-endian signed length + UTF-8 EDN of `[address payload]` — is the same *form* as v1 but argued, not inherited: F3's three truncation categories are exactly prefix arithmetic; EDN is the only tri-host codec with structural round-trip; and integrity rests on the address-hash check (B6/F4), not the codec. The docstring carries this argument including the rejection.
- **(b) Two of my own bugs, both instructive.** First: raw appends of `encode-record` output were missing the length prefix (in the new layout `encode-record` yields record bytes only), and the scanner **correctly truncated my own writes as torn tails** — the first live evidence the F3 code works. Second: `int32-be` multiplied already-shift-amount values by eight again (`(* 8 %)` over `[24 16 8 0]`), producing garbage prefixes; I localized it by a REPL repro in which a hand-built frame worked while the helper's didn't. Both fixed; the `frame-of` helper now exists precisely to compose prefix + record bytes.
- **(c) Implementation shape.** All host code is confined to eight tiny per-host primitives (`open-file!`, `file-length!`, `read-int!`, `read-bytes!`, `truncate-file!`, `append-frame!`, `sync-file!`, `close-file!`), with **one** shared truncation scan and **one** shared replay loop — deliberately eliminating the three duplicated defrecords of v1 (the drift hazard I flagged in earlier plan reviews). Host notes: cljd's `FileMode.append` is read+write+create (reads allowed — v1 relied on this and it holds); Dart's `getInt32`/`setInt32` default to big-endian, so no endian argument appears anywhere; Node's `"a+"` open makes positional writes append regardless of the offset passed, harmless because the only writer computes the end offset under the put lock.
- **(d) My prime suspect if the six Dart failures are mine.** The cljd arm of `host-bytes` is `(typed/Uint8List.fromList ints)` where `frame-of` feeds it `(concat …)` — a **lazy seq**, and `Uint8List.fromList` wants an indexable List; whether ClojureDart's interop accepts a lazy seq there is exactly the kind of thing that fails only on the Dart lane (clj's `byte-array` accepts any seq, which is why clj was green). Suspect #2 is `(.-length bs)` on the cljd returns. Both are one-line fixes (`vec` the concat) if confirmed — but I was killed before confirming, so treat this as a lead, not a diagnosis.
- **(e) With more budget:** a unit-level `frame-of` round-trip check (encode → frame-of → the backend's own scanner reads exactly one frame), which would have caught bug (b) at the unit level instead of through seven integration failures; and a fourth `records` consumer check on the *rewritten* file after a torn-tail truncation while another handle holds it open.
- **(f) Small ergonomic fact:** `thrown?` requires a literal class or keyword — a `(thrown-on-host)` function wrapper does not compile; the sites inline `#?(:clj Exception :cljs :default :cljd Object)`, per the repo's existing convention.

Nothing was staged or committed; scope was respected exactly (the two rewrites, the two deletions, one stale comment in `btree_durability_test.cljc`); and the consumer check (`btree_durability_test`, `dao.space.*`, `dao.jing-test`) passed unchanged on both hosts I completed.
